{
 "summary": "Ensure block data I/O errors are handled cleanly instead of being silently swallowed or triggering hard assertions. It updates FlatFileSeq::Open so that parent directory creation failures return nullptr rather than throwing an unhandled filesystem exception, and replaces an assert in GETBLOCKTXN message handling with a fatal graceful shutdown. Extensive unit and functional tests are added to verify filesystem failure behavior across block connection, reorgs, RPCs, and P2P requests.",
 "problem": "When the blocks directory becomes inaccessible (such as during a detached storage volume or permissions change), FlatFileSeq::Open throws a filesystem_error. When this occurs during ActivateBestChain, the exception bypasses FatalError and is swallowed by the generic P2P catch-all with only net-debug logging, leaving the node appearing alive but permanently stuck and unable to advance the chain. Additionally, GETBLOCKTXN crashes via assertion on read errors instead of initiating a graceful shutdown.",
 "evidence": [
  "PR description: 'If it happens during block connection (due to the unguarded FlatFileSeq::Open -> fs::create_directories call), after AcceptBlock(), inside ActivateBestChain(), the error gets thrown and swallowed by the P2P general try-catch, leaving the node in a living but stuck state.'",
  "Commit 8a3ec93d54: 'swallowing the error msg for any regular user, and every subsequent block arrival retries the same failing read, leaving the node looking healthy while the block processing mechanism is stuck.'"
 ],
